  "findings": [
    "The test set has 149 lost-item queries. Each query is ranked against 298 found records that the classifiers never trained on. A search counts as successful when the correct record appears in the top 5.",
    "CLIP with location and time reaches Recall@5 of 88.6% (MRR 0.619). CLIP visual similarity alone reaches 13.4%. The CNN plus linear classifier reaches 94.0%. Random ranking reaches 1.7%. An oracle that already knows the true category and colour reaches 98.7%.",
    "Adding place and time lifts CLIP above visual similarity alone. The validation search set the weights to 0.45 visual, 0.30 place, and 0.25 time. Many photos share a colour name, so the short text prompt cannot pick one record by itself, and place and time break those ties.",
    "The CNN plus linear classifier is ahead of CLIP on this split. Once the photo is reduced to category and colour, the learned weights can lean on place and time. That decision is easy to explain, and it can only use the colour names seen in training.",
    "In the linear matcher, category agreement raises the probability (weight 4.32); colour agreement raises the probability (weight 0.68); location distance lowers the probability (weight -3.06); time difference lowers the probability (weight -6.87).",
    "On held-out photos, the category softmax reaches 96.6% accuracy and the colour softmax reaches 53.0%. Colour labels come from an automatic dominant-colour reading, so backgrounds can make the labels noisy. The bottle photos are synthetic product shots, and the key set is small, so similar items remain hard to separate.",
    "Campus place and time are simulated, which matches the data plan in the proposal. The system runs locally, and the public photos do not include student cards or faces. This version covers keys, water bottles, earphones, laptops, and watches. Phones and charging cables from the proposal are still missing."
  ]
